Title: Debugging inside a <constructor> function no longer ends the program

The engine calls the wrapper that it generates for every <constructor> function without a Self. The debugger
expects every frame to have one, and ends verse.exe as soon as it lists a call stack with such a frame in it: after
stepping into a constructor function, at a breakpoint inside one, or in any function it calls. This patch treats
the wrapper like a function that takes no Self, so the call stack and the variables show normally.

diff --git a/Engine/Source/Runtime/CoreUObject/Private/VerseVM/VVMDebugger.cpp b/Engine/Source/Runtime/CoreUObject/Private/VerseVM/VVMDebugger.cpp
index 0f9f40794805..6710d40c9d82 100644
--- a/Engine/Source/Runtime/CoreUObject/Private/VerseVM/VVMDebugger.cpp
+++ b/Engine/Source/Runtime/CoreUObject/Private/VerseVM/VVMDebugger.cpp
@@ -140,11 +140,8 @@
 		}
 		FRegisters Registers;
 		VValue SelfValue = Frame->Registers[FRegisterIndex::SELF].Get(Context);
-		V_DIE_IF_MSG(
-			SelfValue.IsUninitialized(),
-			"`Self` should have been bound by now for methods, and set to `GlobalFalse()` for functions. "
-			"This indicates either a codegen issue, or a failure in `CallWithSelf`!");
-		if (IsFalse(SelfValue))
+		// VersePlayground: the wrapper of a <constructor> function is called with no Self, so treat it as a function.
+		if (SelfValue.IsUninitialized() || IsFalse(SelfValue))
 		{
 			Registers.Reserve(Frame->Procedure->NumRegisterNames);
 		}
